### Multicast Adaptive Bit-Rate (MABR): Solution, Footprint, Facts, Hurdles and Evolution
*Presented by Guillaume Bichot*  
*Slides:* [Multicast Adaptive Bit-Rate (MABR): solution, footprint, facts, hurdles and evolution](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-mboned-multicast-adaptive-bit-rate-mabr-solution-footprint-facts-hurdles-and-evolution-02)

* **Architecture & Standards:** MABR leverages DVB-MABR standards using transport protocols such as mSync, FLUTE, ROUTE, and NORM. A multicast server fetches HTTP ABR segments (HLS/DASH) and sends them to an agent in a set-top box or CPE, which acts as a local HTTP proxy/cache for player endpoints.
* **Operational Scale:** Over 25 commercial deployments reaching ~25 million viewers globally. During major events (e.g., FIFA World Cup), MABR offloads over 80% of video delivery traffic from unicast to multicast.
* **Operational Hurdles:** Challenges include CPE integration, low-latency CMAF tuning, and operator constraints (e.g., ISP restrictions on IPv6 or number of multicast groups). Guillaume noted an incident where a post-match surge of users synchronously switching back to unicast web platforms overwhelmed CMS/F5 middleboxes.
* **Evolution:** Ongoing work includes MoDE (Multicast-assisted Unicast Delivery) using edge/host proxies, Open MABR (exposing APIs to allow 3rd-party CDNs to utilize operator multicast networks), and DVB file casting specifications.
* **Discussion:**
  * Jake Holland asked about Wi-Fi delivery; Guillaume clarified MABR is primarily deployed over dedicated managed links to set-top boxes, avoiding Wi-Fi multicast issues.
  * Max Franke inquired about the 80% offload metric; Guillaume confirmed 80% represents a typical average offload efficiency across deployments.
  * Omar Ramadan asked about TR-069 configuration and AMT. Guillaume explained AMT is rarely adopted by ISPs for MABR because operators limit multicast delivery strictly to their own subscribers within walled-garden networks.
  * Mike McBride asked about Source-Specific Multicast (SSM) vs. Any-Source Multicast (ASM) and ad insertion handling. Guillaume confirmed SSM is preferred, and Server-Side Ad Insertion (SSAI) is typically handled on the unicast manifest side.

