### Native Multicast Reception for the Open Web & Scaling the Linux Kernel AMT Relay
*Presented by Omar Ramadan*  
*Slides:* [Native Multicast Reception for the Open Web](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-mboned-native-multicast-reception-for-the-open-web-00) & [Scaling the Linux Kernel AMT Relay and Adding IPv6](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-mboned-scaling-the-linux-kernel-amt-relay-and-adding-ipv6-00)

* **Open Web Multicast API:**
  * Direct Sockets in Chrome currently require isolated web apps or extensions. The proposed W3C/IETF initiative aims to provide web pages with an API to receive authenticated UDP multicast streams directly.
  * Addressing security concerns: Classical TLS pairwise confidentiality does not apply directly to multicast. The proposal focuses on source authentication (DNSSEC, asymmetric signatures) and user consent mechanisms while addressing privacy considerations outlined in `draft-ietf-mboned-multicast-security`.
  * Kyle Rose, Toerless Eckert, and Jake Holland discussed security trade-offs, group keying (GDOI), and k-anonymity models for user privacy.
* **Linux Kernel AMT Relay Scaling:**
  * Recent kernel contributions added hardening, performance optimizations, and initial IPv6 support.
  * Resolved a NAT bug where tunnel sessions were improperly keyed on client IP alone rather than IP and port.
  * Achieved a 30% performance boost by eliminating unnecessary payload copying, successfully scaling to 100,000 concurrent tunnels and maxing out a 100 Gbps interface in testing.

